(
  /* The full view mode is the SETTINGS SCREEN'S BODY — and a workspace has none.

     A VIEW MODE EMITS NO LAYOUT (BB-378, `designbook/design-system/layout.md` §2). It displays an
     entity; the SCREEN arranges one. So this mapping no longer emits a `page_header`: the settings
     screen owns the canvas and owns the `page_header` that carries the screen's single <h1>, the
     inline-editable name inside it and the composed billing sub-line beneath it. That is the same
     rule `bb_ticket.bb_ticket.full.jsonata` and `bb_team.bb_team.full.jsonata` follow, and
     applying it here is what makes the three agree.

     AND THAT LEAVES THIS VIEW MODE EMPTY, WHICH IS A FACT ABOUT THE ENTITY RATHER THAN AN OMISSION.
     A workspace has five fields, and after the rule none of them is body:

       name              the screen's heading — and, because `name` is reversible, the heading IS the
                         field: `action-pattern.md` › *Inline edit* puts a `bb_theme:form_input`
                         (BB-420 Task 31 retired `text_line`) in the `page_header`'s heading slot
                         and the commit IS the write. No save button, no
                         per-field dirty state, no confirmation, and therefore no edit route and no
                         edit dialog for a workspace — `project-create` stays the only dialog in this
                         tree.
       billing_profile   the screen's sub-line, composed. `billing_profile` is an untyped object at
                         the TENANT level, and its meaning there is the CONTRACT or SUBSCRIPTION this
                         tenant is served under — deliberately the same field name as
                         `bb_group.billing_profile`, which means the customer's cost centre one
                         level down. Composing contract · subscription · seats · renewal into one line
                         is what makes the difference between the two levels visible on the screen
                         instead of only in the model.
       created, changed  NOT rendered: machine-owned timestamps with no control beside them, and
                         `changed` in particular is a fact that is stale the moment it paints — the
                         screen would be reporting its own last save.
       owner_user_id     NOT rendered here. The workspace has no members surface (membership lives on
                         teams and projects), so the owner has no row to be one of on this screen.

     So this mapping emits an empty body, and the settings screen places NO workspace node: a node
     that renders nothing is worse than no node, because the next reader cannot tell whether the
     screen forgot the content or the entity has none. The file stays, and says so, precisely so that
     nobody re-adds the header here — which is where it was, and which is the drift BB-378 came back
     to fix.

     WHAT WOULD PUT CONTENT BACK. A field on the workspace that is neither its name nor its billing
     line — the owner as a row, a locale, a retention setting — becomes a `zone` here, and the
     settings screen then places this view mode in `canvas.main` beneath its `page_header`, exactly as
     the ticket detail and the team screen do. Until then there is nothing to display.

     Note what does NOT belong here: the Labels field group on the settings screen is
     `bb_term.bb_labels`, a second entity administered on the same surface, not a field of the
     workspace. It stays a sibling in `canvas.main`.

     THE WORKSPACE ENTITY DOES NOT EXIST IN CODE. No `BbWorkspace.php` exists at any ref of this
     repository — it is owned by BB-316 on a branch that is not on develop — and the screen renders
     anyway, from `data-model.yml` and `designbook/data/` alone. That is the designbook working as
     intended: design leads the backend. */
  []
)
